# Summary
Introduced bug: the new live transfer flow lacks a real drain/lease-expiry/in-flight-operation barrier between fencing and exporting source metadata. This can lead to metadata rollback or loss for writes racing with an operator-triggered live non-overlap PG migration.
The new `control-plane-transfer-pg-metadata-live` path treats `fence_pg_for_metadata_transfer` as a complete quiescence barrier. However, the fence only changes the control-plane PG state to `Peering`; it does not wait for storage nodes/frontends that still have the previous active runtime map or already-open/in-flight RPCs to stop issuing old-epoch metadata commands. The transfer command then immediately snapshots the source retained log, computes a destination proof, switches the acting set, and imports that artifact. A write that races after the exported source state is read but before all old active-route operations are rejected can be acknowledged on the old source but be absent from the transferred artifact, causing the destination PG to activate with metadata that omits the write. The export code also reads replica state, pending slot state, and retained log entries as separate operations without a migration-wide barrier that prevents a concurrent command from being installed after the snapshot point.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm the live migration command fences and immediately exports/imports without an explicit drain, lease-expiry wait, or in-flight barrier.
- [x] Confirm `fence_pg_for_metadata_transfer` only changes control-plane PG state to Peering and does not revoke already-issued Active runtime maps.
- [x] Confirm stale Active route maps remain locally valid until their validity deadline and can still authorize/apply metadata commands.
- [x] Demonstrate an export snapshot can be taken at log index N while a stale Active route later commits log index N+1 on the old source.
- [x] Demonstrate destination import accepts the artifact at log index N and lacks the post-export source metadata write.
## Report
Validated the finding as a semantic data-loss race, not a memory-safety crash. Crash/Valgrind validation was not applicable/available: `valgrind` and `gdb` were absent in the container, but I built the Rust test target with debug info and used LLDB non-interactively.

Key code evidence: `crates/argmin-s3/src/main.rs:583-600` fences the PG and immediately builds a source cluster/export artifact; `main.rs:615-632` then installs the destination acting set and imports that previously exported artifact. `crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:1670-1695` shows `fence_pg_for_metadata_transfer` only transitions Active to Peering via `set_pg_state`; it does not wait for frontend/storage-node route-map expiry or in-flight metadata commands. Runtime-map validity is lease based (`control_plane.rs:546-552`) and local stale maps only reject after validity expiry (`cluster/local.rs:1718-1730`). The export path reads state/pending/log entries and builds the artifact from that snapshot (`cluster.rs:2373-2407`).

Dynamic PoC: I added a focused unit test `live_metadata_transfer_without_drain_can_omit_stale_frontend_write_poc` in `crates/storage/src/cluster/local/tests/command_recovery.rs`. It creates three views over the same local stores: (1) an old Active source route with unexpired validity for node 0, (2) a fenced Peering source route used for export, and (3) a non-overlapping Peering destination route on nodes 1 and 2. The test writes command/log index 1, exports the artifact, then writes command/log index 2 through the still-valid stale Active route. Importing the artifact into the destination succeeds with applied_log_index 1; the second bucket exists on the old source at applied_log_index 2 but is absent from destination nodes, demonstrating acknowledged metadata loss/rollback.

Reproduction output: `cargo test -p storage live_metadata_transfer_without_drain_can_omit_stale_frontend_write_poc -- --nocapture` produced `test ... ok` with `1 passed`. LLDB was run with a breakpoint at the post-export stale write line (`command_recovery.rs:1993`), and the trace shows the test stopped exactly at the stale Active write after artifact export, then continued and passed.

